Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:07.100
Okay, before we get started with this video,
I had one little announcement to make 
but I forgot what it was ....

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.020
Oh, yeah.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.600
As you can see, we have reached 10,000 subscribers 
on the Langfocus channel.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.600
Thank you for helping me reach 10,000.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.000
Let's keep it going. Let's hit 50,000; 100,0000. 
Let's see how big this gets.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.760
But you guys are the original crew,
thank you for your support from day one.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.500
So let's get into today's topic. 
I'm going to talk about the Persian language.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.920
It's sometimes known as Farsi 
but I'm going to call it Persian.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.640
Persian is a language that is widely spoken 
in parts of the Middle East and in Central Asia

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.820
and it has a long history stretching back thousands 
of years with a few different stages along the way.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.280
The Persian language originated in southwestern Persia.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.820
Wait, I don't see Persia on this map right here!

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.560
Oh that's right! Today, Persia is known as Iran!

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.340
Persian is the one official language of Iran and it is 
the native language of about half of Iranians.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.180
The statistics actually range from about 45 to 55% 
but I'm going to say "half".

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.460
and that makes it 38.5 million people.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.000
But virtually, all other Iranians can speak
Persian as a second language as well.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.460
Persian is also an official language in 2 other countries 
but you might not know that

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.960
because they refer to the language 
by different names in those places.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.340
In Afghanistan, the language is referred to 
as "Dari" for political reasons.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.620
but speakers of Dari Persian themselves
often called their language "Farsi".

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.880
It's actually really hard to get reliable statistics 
about Afghanistan, but most sources seem to say that

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.320
it is spoken by 25 to 30 percent 
of the population in Afghanistan.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.060
but it is also widely used as a lingua franca
and pretty much everyone in the country can speak Dari.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.880
In Tajikistan, Persian is referred to as "Tajik" because that's
what the Soviet Union conquerors decided to call it.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.960
You know how foreign empires 
like to divide and conquer.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:01.740
No, no, don't be friends with that powerful country over there. 
No, no, you're separate, you're distinct from them.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.120
But Tajik Persian is indeed a variety of Persian.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.620
It is spoken by 6 million people in Tajikistan

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.640
and another 1.5 million people in the nearby 
Central Asian countries, especially in Uzbekistan.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.080
Persian is also spoken by about 5 million expatriates 
around the world, especially in the Middle East countries,

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.600
like Iraq, UAE, Bahrain, places like that,
as well as the USA.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:28.200
So let's recap: in Iran, there are about 
38.5 million native speakers of Persian.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.720
In Afghanistan and Pakistan, there are 
11 million native speakers of Dari Persian.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.320
In Tajikistan and surrounding areas, there are 
7.5 million native speakers of Tajik Persian

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.920
and there are about five million expat
speaking Persian around the world.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.400
that comes to a total of 62 million native speakers.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:54.280
But if we include all the second language speakers of Persian, 
who use it as a lingua franca, then the number rises 
to 110 million speakers around the world.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.840
Are Persian, Dari and Tajik all really the same language?

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.360
Yes, they are varieties of one single language, 
they are mutually intelligible,

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.000
especially among educated speakers and
the formal languages are very close to each other.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:14.600
There are some differences in the casual language, 
especially in the accent and some of the vocabulary 
that's used, some of the spelling, things like that.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.180
but basically, they are one language 
and they are mutually intelligible.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.240
One difference that I often come across 
is the loan words in the different varieties.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.540
So, in Iran there are more French loanwords.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.860
In Afghanistan, there are more English loan words 
and in Tajikistan, there are more Russian loan words.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.260
Some people who are not so familiar with the Persian language 
might assume that it's related to the Arabic language

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.420
because Iran is in the Middle East and is next to Iraq.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.780
But actually, Persian is in a totally different language family 
than the Arabic language.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.440
It is in the Indo-european language family.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:53.000
That means that Persian shares historical origins 
with most of the languages in Europe including English
as well as the languages of northern India.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:59.480
And more speakers of Indo-european languages find 
Persian much easier to learn that Arabic,
because of the grammatical similarity.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:07.040
But it does look like Arabic because, for historical reasons, 
Persian is usually written in the Arabic script,
except in Tajikistan.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.560
Let's look at the history.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:15.640
First, there was Old Persian
The oldest known records of Old Persian are in inscriptions,
dating back to the first Persian Empire.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:21.200
These writings are written in Babylonian cuneiform script,
which is one of the oldest writing forms in the world.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.060
We have writings like this from various places 
that were part of the first Persian Empire including:

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.420
Iran, of course, but also Romania, Armenia, 
Iraq, Bahrain, Turkey and Egypt.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.820
Next, there was Middle Persian,
from about 300 BCE until about 800 CE.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.980
Old Persian developed into Middle Persian,
which is also known as Pahlavi.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:47.300
We can find Middle Persian, mainly in religious texts 
of the Zoroastrian religion from before the Islamic Period.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:53.660
The language was referred to as Parsig or Parsik 
during this time period because it was the language 
of the Parsa people.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:59.600
The Persian language entered a mysterious new stage 
with the Islamic conquest in 651 AD.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.360
The following 200 years are referred to, 
by Iranian scholars, as "two centuries of silence".

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.820
Because there is very little, almost no literature
available from that time period.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:16.780
We don't know exactly why, but it's possible that
it was forbidden to write in the Persian language 
by the Arab rulers.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:21.940
These two centuries of silence 
are a kind of transition period 
from Middle Persian to Modern Persian.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.820
And Persian was affected by the Arabic language 
quite a lot during this time.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.360
That brings us to Modern Persian 
or New Persian around 800 CE.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.260
The Persian language was in danger 
of being lost and replaced by Arabic,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.260
but Persia was one of the few places where 
the Arabic language did not replace the local language.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:47.940
However, Arabic was used by the intellectuals 
and by the elite and it definitely had a big impact on 
the vocabulary of the Modern Persian that was emerging.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:54.280
After the end of Arab rule in Persia in the ninth century, 
the Persian language and culture was once again reinvigorated.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.600
And a new period of classic literature &amp; poetry arose.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.340
One important piece of literature was Ferdowsi's
"Shahnameh", the "Book of Kings",

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.240
which is the longest work of epic poetry ever written.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.720
Actually, most sources say the Mahabharata is the longest.
So maybe the Shahnameh is #2.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:17.280
It was so influential on future literature and cultural identity 
that the Persian language today remains
relatively unchanged from that time.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.960
This one epic poem is a sort of pillar 
of the Persian language.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.860
It is said that Ferdowsi intentionally limited 
the number of Arabic loan words used in his epic poem,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.060
in order to help preserve the integrity of the Persian language.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.680
Of course, there still are a significant number of Arabic 
words in Persian, but not as many as there could have been.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.880
During this Persian cultural resurgence, the Persian 
language and culture impacted a wide geographic area.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.780
Person became the official language of the Seljuk Empire, 
extending into present-day Turkey

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.360
and later, was the first official language of the Ottoman Empire.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:57.380
Turkish later became the official language  of 
the Ottoman Empire but its vocabulary had been 
very influenced by Arabic and Persian.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:03.340
Persian also spread to the Indian subcontinent, where
it was widely spoken until the arrival of the British.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:09.600
Persian has left its influence on a lot of different languages,
including a heavy influence on the vocabulary of Urdu

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.460
and also some influence on the vocabulary of Turkish.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:18.120
But you can find words of Persian origin in languages 
as far away as the Malay language in Malaysia.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.240
And, of course, there are English words
that are of Persian origins.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:27.760
Some of them came directly into English from Persian
but most of them came indirectly into English,
via a different language:

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:40.820
Bazaar, bronze, jackal, kiosk, lemon, mogul, mummy, 
pijama, scarlet, serendipity, shawl, tulip, turban, typhoon,
khaki, caravan, paradise

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.260
The word checkmate actually comes from 
"Shah Mat", which means "The king is dead".

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.020
And maybe "tiger", though that one is disputed.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:53.920
Learning Persian is a great idea because you will be able
to speak with a hundred and ten million Persian-speakers 
around the world.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:08:01.340
But it also gives you access to a rich history of literature
and insight into a culture that has had a big impact 
on the world.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:08.160
And, if you ever decide to learn Arabic in the future, 
after learning Persian, then you already know 
a lot of Arabic words from Persian.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:11.880
Thank you for watching the LangFocus channel.
I hope that was educational.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.700
If you have any comments, write them down below 
and have a nice day!

